.TH HEAD 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
head \- copy the first lines of files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B head
.RB [ \-n
.IR number ]
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B head
copies the first
.I number
lines of each
.I file
to standard output, in the order the operands are given. If a file
contains fewer than
.I number
lines, it is copied in its entirety.
.PP
When more than one
.I file
operand is given, each file's output is preceded by a banner line of
the form
.RS
.nf
==> \fIfile\fR <==
.fi
.RE
with a blank line before every banner after the first, so the
concatenated output of several files stays visually separated.
No banner is printed when there is exactly one operand (or none).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-n " number"
Copy the first
.I number
lines of each file, instead of the default of 10.
.I number
must be a positive decimal integer; zero, a negative value, or
anything non-numeric is a usage error, not a silent "print nothing"
or "print everything".
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file. If
.B \-
is given, standard input is read at that point in the sequence \(em
so
.B \-
is honored positionally and can appear anywhere in a mixed operand
list, the same convention
.BR cat (1)
uses. If no
.I file
operand is given at all, standard input is used.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
.B head
stops reading a file as soon as its
.I number
th line has been written; it does not drain the rest of the input.
On a pipe, this means the writer at the other end may see the
remainder of its data refused with a broken-pipe error, exactly as
with any other
.BR head (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All input files were copied successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (a bad
.B \-n
argument, or a file that could not be opened or read); processing
continues with the next operand.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tail (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR wc (1)
